# Independent primary-source double coding

This annotation reads only the supplied `WORKINGS.md`, `source_map.json`, and 25 raw RBI HTML pages in the isolated source snapshot. No implementation, prior labels, reviews, plans, memory, chat, or Git history informed the coding. The scope is 13 decisions from 9 October 2024 to 7 October 2026, rather than a research verdict. All supplied raw-source SHA-256 hashes matched. Source display publication dates also agree with the navigation map.

`annotations.json` records committee outcomes and 78 member-meeting records. `statement_evidence.json` preserves the 72 available personal statements with exact original HTML excerpts, source hashes, publication dates, character locations, and normalized readable text. Zero-based paragraph indices are HTML `<p>` positions, not the numbered paragraphs printed by RBI. The standalone `<span>` heading for Poonam Gupta in August 2026 is handled explicitly. `source_snapshot/` preserves a copy of the supplied inputs for reproducibility.

Literal rate ballots, final rate magnitude, rate direction, explicit stance-vote narratives, collective stance-decision narratives, committee-reported views, personal final stance preferences, prior reservations, and guidance qualifications are separate fields. Rate changes are signed basis points. Exact-rate and direction disagreements are calculations from documented final choices. A discussed ambition for a 50-basis-point cut is not substituted for a member's final 25-basis-point choice.

The June 2025 vote table asks whether to reduce the rate and separately gives the magnitude. Every cell is literally “Yes”; Bhattacharya chooses 25 bps while the other five choose 50 bps. Consequently, the supplied minutes contain three literal “No” rate ballots across the panel but four exact-rate disagreements. The fourth is a magnitude disagreement within the common cut direction. April 2025 Bhattacharya's prior reservations about an accommodative stance ended in concurrence; his qualification against a predetermined easing path remains a distinct guidance channel.

Formal stance terminology is deliberately conservative. October and December 2024 attribute neutral-stance votes to all named members, and February 2025 explicitly reports a unanimous neutral-stance vote. No source supplies a separate literal Yes/No stance table. Later “decided”, “agreed”, and “were of the view” wording is preserved as separate evidence rather than silently promoted to a literal ballot. August 2025 says all members decided to remain neutral. October 2025 names Kumar and Singh as preferring accommodative, December 2025 and February 2026 name Singh, and April 2026 Singh expressly changes his own position back to neutral. Whether a downstream protocol treats every official collective decision narrative as a formal vote should be declared before scoring; the separate fields permit either definition without losing the text.

The 7 October 2026 resolution reports a unanimous 25-bps hike to 5.50% and a calibrated-tightening stance, with Kumar and Singh reported to prefer retaining neutral. It contains no literal individual rate-vote table or personal statements; the supplied source map has no minutes source. Each final member rate choice is therefore attributed from the explicit unanimity and supplied roster, while literal ballots and personal stance statements remain null. The two named neutral views are retained in the committee-reported-preference channel. No stance ballot is manufactured for the other four members.

Reservation and guidance passages received a targeted positive-evidence review, not a comprehensive negative annotation of every statement. Identified passages have `true` and exact evidence; all remaining entries are `null` with `not_comprehensively_assessed`. A null must not enter a denominator as an observed negative. General macroeconomic risk descriptions are not automatically disagreements. Full statements are retained to support a later independently defined annotation pass.

Verification passed: 25 source hashes, 13 decisions, 78 member records, 72 literal rate ballots, 72 complete personal-statement records, and 186 checks that manually selected personal evidence falls within the correctly attributed statement. Every evidence fragment is checked against its original decoded HTML slice. Source labels establish the content of these official texts; they establish no empirical model superiority, independent live-model evidence, or external policy validity.

Two exploratory helper failures are retained in `annotations.json`: BeautifulSoup was unavailable (`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named bs4`), and an initial text dump was truncated because it included script/style data. The final parser uses Python's standard-library `HTMLParser`, excludes those data, extracts narrower passages, and preserves complete statement evidence. No package was installed.

